+# Live game: design notes
+
+A **game** is one play of a quiz: a rehearsal, the real talk, the next conference.
+Each game has its own join link, players and answers. The moderator runs it from a
+big-screen page; the audience answers on phones. Nothing here calls an outside
+service: everything lives under `storage/`.
+
+## Flow
+
+1. Quiz page: **Start a game** creates the game and opens its big screen
+   (`/moderate/games/:id`, moderator login). The games of the quiz are listed below
+   the questions, one row each, with a status.
+2. **Lobby.** The big screen shows the quiz title, the join link as text
+   (`localhost:3000/play/K7RZX`, scheme left out), the same link as a QR code, and
+   **Players: N**. Phones open the link, type **Your name**, tap **Join**, and see
+   **Hi, Alice!** and "Waiting for the quiz to start…".
+3. **Start** opens the first active question: **Question 1 of 3**, the text and the
+   options. Phones show the options as buttons. A tap submits at once and marks the
+   option **Your answer**; another tap changes it, the last one counts.
+   The big screen counts **Answers: 1 / 2** (players who answered / players in the game).
+  If the quiz has a time per question (see `timer.md`), the big screen also counts it
+  down, and the question closes by itself at 0:00, as if **Finish** had been clicked.
+4. **Finish** closes the question. The big screen shows the answers per option
+   (**N answers**) and marks the correct options **Correct**. Phones show **Correct!**,
+   **Wrong!** or **No answer**, and highlight the right option.
+5. **Next** opens the next active question. On the last question the button is
+   **End quiz**, which ends the game: the big screen and every phone show
+   **The quiz is over**. The join link then shows the same and refuses new players.
+   The big screen then lists the leaderboard, and each phone shows its place and points.
+   The prizes (**Top 3**, **Draw**, **Reset winners**) are picked on the big screen; see `prizes.md`.
+
+Players may join in any phase before the end, including while a question is open.
+A player who joins mid-question can answer that question.
+
+## Phases and buttons
+
+| Phase    | Big screen                        | Button(s)                              | Phones                                 |
+| -------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
+| `lobby`  | title, join link, QR, Players: N  | **Start** (disabled without active questions); **End quiz** only when there are none | join form, or "Waiting for the quiz to start…" |
+| `open`   | question N of M, options, Answers, countdown if timed | **Finish**       | question and option buttons (no clock) |
+| `closed` | question N of M, counts, Correct  | **Next**, or **End quiz** on the last  | result: Correct! / Wrong! / No answer  |
+| `ended`  | The quiz is over, the leaderboard | **Top N**, **Draw**, **Reset winners** | The quiz is over, Place N of M, points, "You won!" for winners |
+
+Moves are compare-and-set: the phase is read again inside the transaction, so a
+second click on **Finish** (or two moderators) does nothing, and the moderator is
+told "The game has already moved on. Look at the screen."
+
+## Data
+
+- `games`: `quiz_id`, `title` (copied), `code` (5 characters, unique, no 0/O/1/I),
+  `phase`, `position` (question on screen; 0 in the lobby), `revision`, `progress`,
+  `ended_at`.
+- `game_questions`, `game_options`: a **copy** of the quiz's *active* questions and
+  options, made when the game is created (`Game.create_from_quiz!`). Editing or
+  deleting questions later changes neither a running game nor a played one. Inactive
+  questions are not copied, so games skip them and "of M" counts only active ones.
+  Each game question also stores its time limit (`time_limit_seconds`), read from the quiz
+  when the question opens; see `timer.md`.
+- `players`: `game_id`, `name` (1 to 40 characters, trimmed), and `points`, `speed_micros`,
+  `place` and `winner`, which are set when the game ends (see `prizes.md`).
+- `answers`: one row per player per question (unique). Picking again updates it.
+  An answer is only accepted while its question is open, and only for an option
+  of that question. `picked_at` is the time of the last pick (the speed ranking uses it).
+
+Deleting a quiz deletes its games, their players and answers.
+
+## Players and the browser
+
+The browser remembers its player in the session cookie: `session[:players]` maps a
+game's code to the player's id. Opening the join link again continues as the same
+player. Each game has its own entry (up to 20 games per browser), so a browser can
+play two games. Another browser is another player.
+
+## Who sees what (the secret)
+
+The correct options are never sent to a phone or to the big screen before their
+question is closed:
+
+- the phone's view (`play/_state`) renders no correct flags while the question is open;
+- the countdown is drawn only on the big screen (`moderate/games/_screen`). Phones get no
+  clock and no deadline (see `timer.md`);
+- the big screen (`moderate/games/_screen`) renders counts and "Correct" only when closed;
+- the state endpoint `GET /play/:code/state` returns the same partial, so it is covered too;
+- the broadcasts carry no content, only a tick (see below).
+- places and points are shown only in the ended branch (`moderate/games/_screen`,
+  `play/_state`). Before the end nothing shows them.
+
+Tests in `test/integration/play_flow_test.rb` check the HTML and the fragment for
+`is-correct`, `data-correct` and "Correct" while a question is open.
+
+## Live updates
+
+Everything is pushed, and everything can be recovered:
+
+- **Push.** Each change sends a tick (`<turbo-stream action="tick">`) over Action
+  Cable (Turbo Streams). Two streams:
+  - `quizzy_game_<id>`: every change (joins, answers, moves). The big screen listens.
+  - `quizzy_game_<id>_play`: moves and changes of winners (start, finish, next, end,
+    Top N, Draw, Reset winners). Phones listen.
+  A room of phones does not refetch on every answer; the big screen does.
+- **Fetch.** A tick makes the live region (`data-controller="live"`) fetch its own
+  HTML: the big screen from `/moderate/games/:id/live`, a phone from `/play/:code/state`.
+  The fetch is conditional: the ETag is the game `revision` (big screen) or
+  `progress` plus the player's own pick (phone), so an unchanged view is a 304.
+  The browser's cache returns the body for a 304. Because the ETag includes the
+  player's pick, a view is never served stale after a tap.
+- **Poll.** Each live region also fetches every ~4 seconds (with jitter). A tick
+  that was lost is caught up within that time. After a failed fetch the interval
+  doubles (up to 12 seconds) and resets on success.
+- **Reconnect.** When a stream subscription (re)connects, the page catches up
+  immediately and once more after 500 ms. The second fetch covers a change sent just
+  before the server registered the new subscription (Action Cable's test adapter
+  registers subscribers asynchronously, and that race showed up in the browser tests).
+- **No lost typing.** Replacing a region keeps the text a user has typed in its
+  fields and their focus (`live_controller.js`).
+
+Lessons, so they are not learned twice:
+
+- Turbo's stream identifier is just the stream name. Action Cable keeps one
+  subscription per identifier, and it ignores a second subscribe with the same one.
+  Navigating from one page to the next (which a tap does) then let the old page's
+  unsubscribe remove the new page's subscription, so the phone stopped getting ticks.
+  Each `turbo_stream_from` therefore gets a random `data-subscription` attribute
+  (`live_subscription` helper), which makes every identifier unique.
+- The poll and the `connected` catch-up are not optional. Without them, a phone on
+  crowded Wi-Fi shows a stale question until the next change.
+- `fresh_when`/`stale?` renders the 304 itself. Calling `render` after it raises a
+  double-render error. Use `render ... if stale?(...)`.
+- Propshaft: `public/assets/.manifest.json` (from `bin/serve` or
+  `assets:precompile`) makes the test environment use the static resolver, which
+  does not know new stylesheets until the next precompile. Delete `public/assets`
+  when adding a stylesheet and running the tests by hand.
+
+## Layout notes
+
+- The big screen (`layouts/screen.html.erb`) uses the design's `cover`, `screen`,
+  `lobby`, `stage-*` classes. Controls sit in the dark `screen-controls` bar.
+- Phones (`layouts/play.html.erb`) use the `play-*` classes.
+- The live region has `display: contents`, so the page layout is unchanged.
+- Counters are `inline-flex` pills. Their text must sit in one inline element
+  (`<span>Players: <strong>2</strong></span>`), or browsers put "Players:" and "2" on
+  separate lines and the text "Players: 2" is no longer found.
+- The join link is built from the request host. The moderator should open the big
+  screen on the address the phones use (for example the laptop's LAN address), not
+  `localhost`, or the phones cannot reach the link.
+
+## Routes
+
+| Method and path | What |
+| --- | --- |
+| `POST /moderate/quizzes/:quiz_id/games` | "Start a game" |
+| `GET /moderate/games/:id` | the big screen |
+| `GET /moderate/games/:id/live` | the big screen's live fragment (ETag) |
+| `POST /moderate/games/:id/start`, `finish`, `next`, `end_quiz` | the buttons |
+| `POST /moderate/games/:id/top_winners`, `draw_winners`, `reset_winners` | the prizes, after the end (see `prizes.md`) |
+| `GET /play/:code` | the join link: join form, or the phone's view |
+| `GET /play/:code/state` | the phone's live fragment (ETag) |
+| `POST /play/:code/join` | "Join" (`player[name]`) |
+| `POST /play/:code/answer` | a tap (`game_option_id`) |
+
+The player routes are public (no login). Unknown codes show "No such quiz" with 404.
+
+## Labels the QA tests rely on
+
+Buttons: "Start a game", "Start", "Finish", "Next", "End quiz", "Join", "Top 3" (N is the
+number of winners), "Draw", "Reset winners".
+Countdown: "M:SS" in `div.countdown` (role timer, aria-label "Time left"), see `timer.md`.
+Fields: "Your name" (label). Texts: "Players: 2", "Join at", the join link, "Question 1 of 3",
+"Answers: 1 / 2", "Correct", "Correct!", "Wrong!", "No answer", "Your answer",
+"Hi, Alice!", "The quiz is over", "Waiting for the quiz to start…", "Winner" (leaderboard
+badge), "You won!", "Place 2 of 5", "3 points", "2 points", "1 point", "0 points".
+Field "Number of winners" (quiz settings).
+
+No confirmation dialog on "End quiz": the flow the moderator is asked to follow is
+Finish, Next, End quiz, and the button is plain, so a dialog would be an extra step.
+
+## Tests
+
+- `test/models/game_test.rb`: the copy, codes, every transition and its refusals,
+  answers (last pick, only the open question, only own game), joins, broadcasts.
+- `test/integration/game_moderation_test.rb`: login on every game route, Start a
+  game, each phase of the big screen, the buttons, ETag on the live fragment.
+- `test/integration/play_flow_test.rb`: join, rejoin, another browser, two games
+  in one browser, late join and the end, answers, the no-leak checks, ETags.
+- `test/system/live_game_test.rb`: a whole game in four browsers (moderator and
+  three phones), a missed update caught up by the poll, typing kept during an
+  update, two games in one browser.
+- The prizes (`prizes.md` lists them): `test/models/leaderboard_test.rb`,
+  `test/models/game_prizes_test.rb`, `test/models/quiz_test.rb`,
+  `test/integration/prizes_test.rb`, `test/system/prizes_test.rb`.
+
+Verified against `bin/serve` in production mode too (solid_cable): a scripted game
+with a moderator and two phones (live counters and results within a fraction of a
+second), and a phone taken offline and back (it catches up within a fraction of a
+second of reconnecting).
